# FreeBird Dash — local configuration.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# `.env` is gitignored; this template is not. Nothing here is required to boot:
# with an empty file the server runs, serves the catalog, and connects to any
# API you describe by hand or import from an OpenAPI spec.
#
# A real environment variable always beats this file, so exporting a value in
# your shell or CI overrides whatever is written here.
#
# The server searches upward from its own directory, so this file works at the
# repo root or in apps/server. The repo root is the conventional place.


# ─── AI provider ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# One key unlocks two things: reading API documentation (ladder rung 3), and
# searching the web by product name (rung 4, via the provider's own server-side
# web search). Anthropic is preferred when both are set.
#
# Without a key, discovery still works for OpenAPI specs and catalog entries —
# those paths are deterministic and need no model at all.

ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
# OPENAI_API_KEY=

# Each AI action runs on the model that suits it, and the defaults are what
# almost everyone should use. Setting up an API — reading its docs, mapping it,
# building a widget — is judgement and runs on the capable model. Using what
# you built — chat, naming fields, matching a phrase — is reading and runs on
# the cheap one. Setup is rare and one-off; use is constant.
#
#   capable   claude-sonnet-5   gpt-4.1
#   fast      claude-haiku-4-5  gpt-4.1-mini
#
# Everything below is optional. Prefer the picker in the UI, which shows what
# each action costs beside the control that changes it.

# Use one model for every action, overriding all of the above. Setting this
# also disables the picker, which says so rather than appearing to do nothing.
# DASH_LLM_MODEL=

# Pin one action. Highest precedence of all — above the picker, above the line
# above. One per action:
#   DASH_MODEL_WIDGET     building a widget: pick the endpoints, bind the fields
#   DASH_MODEL_DISCOVER   working out how to call an API from its docs
#   DASH_MODEL_MAP        finding how an API's resources relate
#   DASH_MODEL_RECORD     choosing what opening a row shows
#   DASH_MODEL_LABEL      turning field names into readable ones
#   DASH_MODEL_CHAT       answering questions about a dashboard
#   DASH_MODEL_NARROW     deciding which real values a word covers
#   DASH_MODEL_SUGGEST    the second opinion on a proposed resource map
# DASH_MODEL_WIDGET=

# Model used for web search specifically, if you want it different from the
# authoring model. Web search needs Opus 4.6+ or Sonnet 4.6+ for the current
# tool; older models fall back to the basic one automatically.
# DASH_SEARCH_MODEL=

# Pin which provider performs web search: anthropic | openai.
# Unset picks the first provider that has a key. There is no fallback when the
# named provider has no key — the rung is simply reported as unavailable.
# DASH_SEARCH_PROVIDER=

# The most one request may spend on model calls, in US dollars. Default: 2.00.
#
# Each thing that reaches for a model is already bounded on its own — a search
# reads at most four sources, a deep read runs at most twenty chunks — but one
# request can do several of those in a row, and nothing bounded the total. An
# ordinary chat turn costs well under $0.20 and the dearest deliberate path, a
# deep read, lands near $0.60, so the default is roughly three times the worst
# case: high enough that normal use never meets it, low enough to stop a loop.
#
# Set it to 0 to switch the ceiling off entirely. A model with no published
# rate in pricing.ts counts as $0 and so cannot trip it — watch the `unpriced`
# figure on the cost line if you have pinned an unusual model.
# DASH_TURN_CEILING_USD=


# ─── Storage ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Where dashboards/ and connections/ are read and written. Defaults to the
# process working directory, which is apps/server under `pnpm --filter`.
# DASH_ROOT=

# Directory of catalog seed entries shipped with the repo. The per-instance
# overlay always lives under .dash/catalog and is not affected by this.
# DASH_CATALOG_DIR=

# Port the API listens on. The web app's dev proxy expects 4600.
# PORT=4600


# ─── Secrets at rest ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 64 hex characters (32 bytes) — the AES-256-GCM key that encrypts the API keys
# you enter in the UI. Leave it unset and a random one is generated into
# .dash/master-key (gitignored) on first boot, which is the right default for
# local use.
#
# WARNING: setting this AFTER connections already exist makes their stored keys
# undecryptable — the vault cannot read what a different key encrypted. Set it
# on a fresh instance, or re-enter each key afterwards.
#
# Generate one with:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
# DASH_MASTER_KEY=

# The second-opinion pass over a connection's suggestions, which reads a
# resource map once per connection and proposes pairings the deterministic
# rules would miss. This is now the `suggest` action and already runs on the
# cheap model, so the name is kept only because it predates that — it is an
# alias for DASH_MODEL_SUGGEST and does exactly the same thing.
# DASH_REVIEW_MODEL=
